WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.304 --> 00:00:07.907
 NARRATOR:
 Deep below the Pacific Ocean

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:11.711
 lies a tectonic monster.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:13.946
MAN: You know how we told you
not to panic?

00:00:13.946 --> 00:00:15.982
Well, that's all off.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.683
Panic. Run for your life.

00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:18.951
(screaming)

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:22.922
 NARRATOR: The world's biggest
 tsunami machine,

00:00:22.922 --> 00:00:27.159
 its dangers hidden from view,

00:00:27.159 --> 00:00:28.862
 until now.

00:00:31.964 --> 00:00:35.968
 Imagine if we could
 empty the oceans,

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:38.170
 letting the water drain away

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:42.242
 to reveal the secrets
 of the seafloor.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:45.644
 Now, we can,

00:00:45.644 --> 00:00:49.415
 using the latest underwater
 scanning technology,

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:51.384
 piercing the deep oceans,

00:00:51.384 --> 00:00:57.090
 and turning accurate data
 into 3D images.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:01.093
 This time, is the world's
 next mega-earthquake

00:01:01.093 --> 00:01:03.429
 brewing
 off the coast of Seattle?

00:01:03.429 --> 00:01:04.830
MAN: We can have the big one

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.298
right now, as we speak.

00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:07.867
 NARRATOR:
 Why has the discovery

00:01:07.867 --> 00:01:12.038
 of an underwater volcano,
 just 44 miles from Tokyo,

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:15.074
 got seismic experts so worried?

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:18.778
 And what do scientists caught
 up in a undersea earthquake

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:22.047
 learn about the dangers
 facing New Zealand?

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:26.052
MAN: The front of it would have
a huge amount of energy.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:28.153
The scale is just immense.

00:01:28.153 --> 00:01:29.322
 NARRATOR: Drain the Oceans

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:33.959
 reveals the secrets
 of the deadly Pacific.

00:01:33.959 --> 00:01:40.866
(music)

00:01:40.866 --> 00:01:47.006
(music)

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:49.041
 The Pacific Ocean--

00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:53.045
 the planet's largest
 and deepest.

00:01:53.045 --> 00:01:57.383
 500 million people
 live around its coast.

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:06.992
 But deep beneath the waves
 lurks a terrifying monster

00:02:06.992 --> 00:02:11.063
 that threatens all of them,

00:02:11.063 --> 00:02:16.502
 the planet's largest and
 most dangerous seismic zone--

00:02:16.502 --> 00:02:20.005
 the Ring of Fire.

00:02:20.005 --> 00:02:24.377
 90 percent of all earthquakes
 strike here

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:26.178
 and when they do,

00:02:26.178 --> 00:02:29.515
 the consequences
 are devastating.

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:30.750
(siren)

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:36.989
(siren)

00:02:36.989 --> 00:02:39.192
 March 11, 2011.

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.827
(speaking Japanese)

00:02:41.827 --> 00:02:44.096
 NARRATOR:
 Tohoku, northeastern Japan.

00:02:44.096 --> 00:02:46.198
(shouting)

00:02:46.198 --> 00:02:52.138
 A magnitude 9 earthquake
 violently shakes the region.

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:55.341
MAN: Oh!

00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:58.344
 NARRATOR: The quake causes
 buildings to collapse

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:02.048
 and people to flee
 for their lives.

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:05.151
(shouting)

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:08.488
 But that's not the end
 of the destruction.

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:15.128
 A defining characteristic
 of the Ring of Fire

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:17.297
 is that
 the biggest earthquakes here

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:21.968
 shake not just the land,
 but the seabed as well...

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:28.841
 ...generating
 huge surges of water...

00:03:28.841 --> 00:03:30.343
(speaking Japanese)

00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:33.212
 NARRATOR: ...deadly tsunamis.

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:37.183
(shouting)

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:40.853
(music)

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:47.560
(music)

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.427
(shouting)

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:54.900
(shouting)

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:59.738
(music)

00:03:59.738 --> 00:04:04.911
(music)

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:09.115
 In Japan, almost 16,000 people
 are killed.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:15.287
 And geologists are certain
 it will happen again

00:04:15.287 --> 00:04:19.358
 somewhere around
 the Pacific Ring of Fire,

00:04:19.358 --> 00:04:22.328
 but when and where?

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:28.400
 Pulling the plug
 on the Pacific Ocean

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.472
 reveals a glimpse of one
 of its most terrifying secrets,

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:36.509
 just off the west coast
 of America--

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:39.278
 not the infamous
 San Andreas Fault

00:04:39.278 --> 00:04:41.247
 in California,

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:43.950
 but something close
 to the teeming cities

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:48.086
 of Portland,
 Seattle, and Vancouver,

00:04:48.086 --> 00:04:50.356
 something far more menacing.

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:55.495
(music)

00:04:55.495 --> 00:04:58.297
LELAND O'DRISCOLL: It's known
that it's an active structure.

00:04:58.297 --> 00:04:59.498
It could happen now, and that's

00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:01.300
truly the source of fear.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:07.039
(music)

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:08.674
 NARRATOR:
 This region of North America

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:12.744
 is known as Cascadia,

00:05:12.744 --> 00:05:17.483
 one of the most beautiful
 and peaceful in North America.

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:20.486
 It's also home
 to 10 million people.

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:27.293
 They feel safe here,
 and why wouldn't they?

00:05:27.293 --> 00:05:30.296
 In all recorded history,
 there's been no trace

00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:34.599
 of any natural disasters
 in the region,

00:05:34.599 --> 00:05:38.470
 but on the 18th of May 1980,

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:41.374
 everything changes.

00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:42.674
(rumbling)

00:05:42.674 --> 00:05:43.742
MAN: There is no question at all

00:05:43.742 --> 00:05:46.646
that the volcanic activity
has begun.

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:51.517
 NARRATOR: Mount St. Helens,
 50 miles from Portland.

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:55.320
 Huge eruptions blast
 a 1,300-foot crater

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:59.492
 in the summit of a volcano
 long thought to be dormant.

00:06:03.295 --> 00:06:05.664
REPORTER: It is the biggest
eruption ever recorded

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:07.499
of Mount St. Helens so far.

00:06:07.499 --> 00:06:08.400
REPORTER:
I think it does not look good

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.469
for people in that area now.

00:06:10.469 --> 00:06:12.338
 NARRATOR: It's the worst
 volcano disaster

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:16.742
 in U.S. history,
 claiming 57 lives.

00:06:16.742 --> 00:06:19.812
(music)

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:24.250
(music)

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:28.820
 Scientists are now forced
 to ask a tough question.

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.490
MAN: Is it, is it scaled
to look at tsunamis?

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:32.691
 NARRATOR: Does the eruption

00:06:32.691 --> 00:06:35.561
 of the supposedly inactive
 Mount St. Helens

00:06:35.561 --> 00:06:38.764
 mean the 10 million inhabitants
 of Cascadia

00:06:38.764 --> 00:06:42.368
 are now sitting
 on a seismic time bomb

00:06:42.368 --> 00:06:45.471
 that just started ticking?

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:49.542
 To find out, scientists
 launch an urgent investigation

00:06:49.542 --> 00:06:53.345
 on land
 and deep under the sea.

00:06:53.345 --> 00:06:57.150
 Off the coast of Cascadia,
 the latest scanning technology

00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:02.521
 reveals something extraordinary
 and terrifying.

00:07:02.521 --> 00:07:06.459
 Based upon their exact data,
 for the first time,

00:07:06.459 --> 00:07:09.561
 we can pull the plug
 on the Pacific Ocean

00:07:09.561 --> 00:07:13.399
 to reveal
 their shocking discovery.

00:07:13.399 --> 00:07:16.134
 As the water drains away,

00:07:16.134 --> 00:07:21.541
 a vast world no human has
 ever seen before is laid bare.

00:07:24.476 --> 00:07:27.412
 For mile after mile
 across its heart,

00:07:27.412 --> 00:07:29.315
 the Pacific Ocean floor

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:33.252
 is a vast, flat,
 featureless plain...

00:07:35.488 --> 00:07:38.691
 ...until suddenly,

00:07:38.691 --> 00:07:40.793
 the landscape changes.

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:45.564
 Here, just 150 miles

00:07:45.564 --> 00:07:49.801
 from the great cities
 of the Pacific Northwest,

00:07:49.801 --> 00:07:53.171
 a dramatic transformation
 takes place,

00:07:53.171 --> 00:07:56.709
 revealing a fractured,
 broken seafloor...

00:07:58.477 --> 00:08:00.946
 ...brooding,

00:08:00.946 --> 00:08:02.882
 menacing.

00:08:02.882 --> 00:08:04.483
O'DRISCOLL: One of the best
analogies I can think of

00:08:04.483 --> 00:08:07.586
is looking
at the, the front end of a car

00:08:07.586 --> 00:08:10.556
that's been
in a head-on collision,

00:08:10.556 --> 00:08:12.825
dented and corroded
and corrugated

00:08:12.825 --> 00:08:15.161
into this complex mess.

00:08:18.564 --> 00:08:21.334
 NARRATOR: The scale of
 this geological fender bender

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:23.436
 is truly epic.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.272
 Colossal cliffs,

00:08:27.272 --> 00:08:30.243
 almost twice the height
 of the Grand Canyon...

00:08:32.544 --> 00:08:36.682
 ...some deep enough to hide
 six Empire State Buildings

00:08:36.682 --> 00:08:40.319
 stacked on top of each other,

00:08:40.319 --> 00:08:45.491
 all of it stretching 700 miles
 along the Cascadian coastline.

00:08:49.595 --> 00:08:52.565
 This titanic rupture
 in the earth's crust

00:08:52.565 --> 00:08:56.502
 is what geologists call
 a subduction zone.

00:09:01.406 --> 00:09:02.775
 At the core of our planet

00:09:02.775 --> 00:09:05.778
 is a boiling cauldron
 of molten iron,

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:11.483
 surrounded
 by a thick mantle of rock.

00:09:11.483 --> 00:09:16.021
 That outer crust is divided
 into huge tectonic plates

00:09:16.021 --> 00:09:18.424
 that are constantly
 in motion.

00:09:21.493 --> 00:09:25.364
 When they collide,
 they thrust mountains upwards

00:09:25.364 --> 00:09:28.534
 and carve deep trenches
 into the ocean floor

00:09:28.534 --> 00:09:33.639
 as one plate drives the other
 back inside the earth.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.242
 If the plates lock together,

00:09:37.242 --> 00:09:40.212
 tremendous pressure
 begins to build.

00:09:43.449 --> 00:09:44.750
O'DRISCOLL:
Only in subduction zones

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:46.585
where the plates
are compressing together

00:09:46.585 --> 00:09:48.587
and there's this lock
between the plates,

00:09:48.587 --> 00:09:50.255
that, those are
the only fault zones

00:09:50.255 --> 00:09:51.390
that have the potential

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:52.691
to create magnitude

00:09:52.691 --> 00:09:54.594
9.0 and higher events.

00:09:56.462 --> 00:09:58.530
 NARRATOR: But that's not all.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:02.334
 Subduction zones,
 because they are underwater,

00:10:02.334 --> 00:10:07.273
 can generate not just
 earthquakes, but huge tsunamis.

00:10:11.309 --> 00:10:15.614
 Written history suggests
 that Cascadia's subduction zone

00:10:15.614 --> 00:10:19.018
 has been inactive
 for centuries,

00:10:19.018 --> 00:10:23.689
 but does written history
 go back far enough?

00:10:23.689 --> 00:10:28.527
 Is this monster really
 as sleepy as it seems?

00:10:28.527 --> 00:10:34.667
(music)

00:10:34.667 --> 00:10:37.702
 Scientists scour
 the landscape around Cascadia

00:10:37.702 --> 00:10:40.272
 in search of answers,

00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:44.242
 and, in the banks
 of coastal rivers like this,

00:10:44.242 --> 00:10:48.246
 they find an alarming pattern.

00:10:48.246 --> 00:10:50.549
DAVID YAMAGUCHI: It's very much
like a chocolate cake.

00:10:50.549 --> 00:10:51.584
You slice a chocolate cake,

00:10:51.584 --> 00:10:53.819
and there's a layer
of brown frosting,

00:10:53.819 --> 00:10:56.856
and then, down below, there's
another layer of brown frosting,

00:10:56.856 --> 00:10:59.858
and the Washington coast
looks very much like that.

00:10:59.858 --> 00:11:01.326
 NARRATOR:
 But there's nothing sweet

00:11:01.326 --> 00:11:04.763
 about these layers
 of peat and sand.

00:11:04.763 --> 00:11:06.632
YAMAGUCHI:
Each of these buried soil layers

00:11:06.632 --> 00:11:08.667
on the Washington coast

00:11:08.667 --> 00:11:12.370
reflected times when the coast
had been jerked downwards

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:15.574
by the occurrence
of an earthquake.

00:11:15.574 --> 00:11:17.376
 NARRATOR:
 In the layers of peat,

00:11:17.376 --> 00:11:21.313
 David Yamaguchi
 and geologist Brian Atwater

00:11:21.313 --> 00:11:24.416
 unearth evidence
 of seismic devastation

00:11:24.416 --> 00:11:27.886
 stretching back
 over thousands of years,

00:11:27.886 --> 00:11:32.558
 quakes that scarred the entire
 coastline of Cascadia.

00:11:32.558 --> 00:11:34.593
 The question now is,

00:11:34.593 --> 00:11:38.798
 when was the last one,
 and how big was it?

00:11:45.571 --> 00:11:48.741
 NARRATOR: The Copalis River,
 Washington State.

00:11:51.643 --> 00:11:56.314
 Scientist David Yamaguchi
 is on a quest.

00:11:56.314 --> 00:11:59.618
 He and his fellow scientists
 already suspect

00:11:59.618 --> 00:12:02.787
 that the 10 million inhabitants
 of Cascadia

00:12:02.787 --> 00:12:06.725
 are living
 on a seismic time bomb,

00:12:06.725 --> 00:12:09.428
 but when did it last go off,

00:12:09.428 --> 00:12:14.032
 and how big was it
 when it did?

00:12:14.032 --> 00:12:19.471
 Three miles inland, he comes
 across a haunting sight.

00:12:19.471 --> 00:12:25.777
(music)

00:12:25.777 --> 00:12:31.750
(music)

00:12:31.750 --> 00:12:35.120
 This is what's left
 of a once mighty forest

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:39.724
 of red cedar
 and spruce trees,

00:12:39.724 --> 00:12:41.894
 thousands of petrified corpses

00:12:41.894 --> 00:12:43.962
 scattered
 over hundreds of miles

00:12:43.962 --> 00:12:46.698
 along Cascadia's coast.

00:12:46.698 --> 00:12:47.700
YAMAGUCHI: And the mystery here

00:12:47.700 --> 00:12:49.935
is what killed the trees,

00:12:49.935 --> 00:12:53.639
and when did they die?

00:12:53.639 --> 00:13:00.379
(chainsaw)

00:13:00.379 --> 00:13:03.749
 NARRATOR:
 Sampling dozens of the trees,

00:13:03.749 --> 00:13:06.852
 Yamaguchi analyzes
 their age rings,

00:13:06.852 --> 00:13:10.222
 and what he finds
 is a new mystery.

00:13:10.222 --> 00:13:13.191
YAMAGUCHI: The trees were fine
and healthy in 1699.

00:13:13.191 --> 00:13:15.027
Then, the following summer,

00:13:15.027 --> 00:13:16.962
the trees that, that would
normally have produced

00:13:16.962 --> 00:13:18.530
another annual ring

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:19.732
were nearly all dead.

00:13:21.500 --> 00:13:24.937
 NARRATOR: What happened
 to kill an entire forest

00:13:24.937 --> 00:13:28.207
 in one instant?

00:13:28.207 --> 00:13:31.543
 Another scientist
 thinks the answer could lie

00:13:31.543 --> 00:13:33.812
 deep in the geological
 car crash

00:13:33.812 --> 00:13:36.181
 of the Cascadia
 subduction zone,

00:13:36.181 --> 00:13:38.583
 just 100 miles away.

00:13:38.583 --> 00:13:43.055
(music)

00:13:43.055 --> 00:13:48.460
 Chris Goldfinger is an
 underwater earthquake detective

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:50.763
 seeking evidence
 in core samples

00:13:50.763 --> 00:13:54.700
 taken from deep
 under the seafloor.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.735
 Studying the layers of sediment

00:13:56.735 --> 00:14:00.572
 reveals when and where
 earthquakes struck in the past

00:14:00.572 --> 00:14:03.976
 and how powerful they were.

00:14:03.976 --> 00:14:05.511
CHRIS GOLDFINGER: Sediment
settles to the seafloor,

00:14:05.511 --> 00:14:07.512
but it got there somehow,

00:14:07.512 --> 00:14:11.516
from some storm, an earthquake,
and even a volcanic eruption

00:14:11.516 --> 00:14:13.752
will leave a deposit
on the seafloor,

00:14:13.752 --> 00:14:17.556
and so it's just
a very precise,

00:14:17.556 --> 00:14:18.791
three-dimensional
tape recorder,

00:14:18.791 --> 00:14:21.126
just recording
every single thing that happens.

00:14:23.528 --> 00:14:24.829
Well, this is a core sample

00:14:24.829 --> 00:14:28.834
from, from the seafloor
off of Cascadia,

00:14:28.834 --> 00:14:32.804
and it's about
2,000 years' worth of sediment.

00:14:32.804 --> 00:14:34.906
 NARRATOR:
 In the middle of the sample,

00:14:34.906 --> 00:14:37.910
 dating to roughly
 300 years ago,

00:14:37.910 --> 00:14:42.647
 Goldfinger finds the classic
 signs of a large earthquake.

00:14:42.647 --> 00:14:44.149
GOLDFINGER: Well, in fact,
the shells like this

00:14:44.149 --> 00:14:46.851
turn out to be
shallow-water fossils,

00:14:46.851 --> 00:14:48.687
but this is a deep-water core,

00:14:48.687 --> 00:14:50.089
so these things
were carried down

00:14:50.089 --> 00:14:52.657
from shallow water
to deep water.

00:14:52.657 --> 00:14:56.828
What this points to is
a fairly dramatic kind of event.

00:14:56.828 --> 00:14:59.297
(rumbling)

00:14:59.297 --> 00:15:02.935
 NARRATOR: That dramatic event
 was an earthquake

00:15:02.935 --> 00:15:07.873
 that triggered
 a vast underwater landslide.

00:15:07.873 --> 00:15:10.675
 And it all happened
 around the same time

00:15:10.675 --> 00:15:14.947
 that Yamaguchi's trees died.

00:15:14.947 --> 00:15:18.750
 Next, he compares core samples
 of the same date,

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:20.786
 taken along the 700 miles

00:15:20.786 --> 00:15:24.022
 of the Cascadia
 subduction zone.

00:15:24.022 --> 00:15:28.193
 Core after core reveals
 the same disturbed layer,

00:15:28.193 --> 00:15:31.897
 all occurring
 at the exact same time.

00:15:35.267 --> 00:15:39.938
 This is evidence
 of no ordinary earthquake,

00:15:39.938 --> 00:15:42.674
 but a monster.

00:15:42.674 --> 00:15:46.078
GOLDFINGER: Cascadia, through
the study of past earthquakes,

00:15:46.078 --> 00:15:48.179
has shown that it's capable
of generating earthquakes

00:15:48.179 --> 00:15:52.985
that run the full length,
roughly 1,000 kilometers.

00:15:52.985 --> 00:15:56.754
 NARRATOR: In 1700,
 the Cascadia subduction zone

00:15:56.754 --> 00:16:00.992
 generated a cataclysmic quake
 along its entire length,

00:16:00.992 --> 00:16:04.295
 with a magnitude that
 Goldfinger and other scientists

00:16:04.295 --> 00:16:07.700
 calculate as level 8.

00:16:10.001 --> 00:16:12.403
 But that's not all.

00:16:12.403 --> 00:16:17.209
 This was more
 than just an earthquake.

00:16:17.209 --> 00:16:22.081
 Japan has a long history
 of earthquakes and tsunamis,

00:16:22.081 --> 00:16:27.019
 with written evidence
 going back 1,400 years.

00:16:27.019 --> 00:16:29.187
 Among these historical records,

00:16:29.187 --> 00:16:32.657
 in accounts written
 by samurai and merchants,

00:16:32.657 --> 00:16:36.828
 researchers uncover reports
 of a 16-foot tsunami

00:16:36.828 --> 00:16:40.832
 that arrives
 from far out in the Pacific,

00:16:40.832 --> 00:16:43.101
 swamping the eastern coast.

00:16:43.101 --> 00:16:44.903
 It destroys houses

00:16:44.903 --> 00:16:48.107
 and causes villagers
 to flee to higher ground.

00:16:49.774 --> 00:16:53.978
 The date of these reports?

00:16:53.978 --> 00:16:56.881
 1700.

00:16:56.881 --> 00:17:01.086
YAMAGUCHI: They propose that
our earthquake was magnitude 9.

00:17:01.086 --> 00:17:03.154
A magnitude 8
was not big enough

00:17:03.154 --> 00:17:05.990
to produce a wave
that's that wide.

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:07.259
In addition,

00:17:07.259 --> 00:17:10.195
based on the arrival times
of the waves in Japan,

00:17:10.195 --> 00:17:11.497
the earthquake
must have happened here

00:17:11.497 --> 00:17:14.866
on January 26, 1700

00:17:14.866 --> 00:17:16.935
at 9:00 at night,

00:17:16.935 --> 00:17:18.170
yes, local time.

00:17:20.138 --> 00:17:23.474
 NARRATOR: The Cascadia
 mega-quake was so powerful

00:17:23.474 --> 00:17:25.109
 that it generated a tsunami

00:17:25.109 --> 00:17:30.149
 that hit Japan,
 4,500 miles away.

00:17:32.884 --> 00:17:34.319
 Using the combined insights

00:17:34.319 --> 00:17:37.723
 of experts from both sides
 of the Pacific

00:17:37.723 --> 00:17:40.158
 and the extraordinary
 drained landscape

00:17:40.158 --> 00:17:42.994
 of the subduction zone,

00:17:42.994 --> 00:17:45.096
 it's now possible to visualize

00:17:45.096 --> 00:17:50.168
 the titanic events
 of January 1700

00:17:50.168 --> 00:17:53.805
 and its deadly trail
 of destruction.

00:17:53.805 --> 00:17:58.010
(music)

00:17:58.010 --> 00:17:59.277
GOLDFINGER:
The entire subduction zone

00:17:59.277 --> 00:18:01.814
shakes very hard.

00:18:04.482 --> 00:18:07.852
The ground motion is big,
and it's over a big area,

00:18:07.852 --> 00:18:10.121
so it's gonna shake
a lot of sediment loose.

00:18:10.121 --> 00:18:15.160
(music)

00:18:15.160 --> 00:18:16.995
There are gonna be
hundreds of landslides.

00:18:16.995 --> 00:18:19.164
They're gonna all
come tumbling down the slope

00:18:19.164 --> 00:18:20.899
and leave a deposit.

00:18:20.899 --> 00:18:26.938
(music)

00:18:26.938 --> 00:18:28.807
(music)

00:18:31.142 --> 00:18:34.345
 NARRATOR: The quake
 also triggers a huge tsunami

00:18:34.345 --> 00:18:39.117
 which smashes
 into the coast of Cascadia,

00:18:39.117 --> 00:18:42.520
 flooding the land
 with seawater,

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.123
 including the red cedar forest

00:18:45.123 --> 00:18:48.093
 and everything else
 in its path.

00:18:52.464 --> 00:18:54.533
 Although
 there is no written history

00:18:54.533 --> 00:18:58.503
 of the 1700 earthquake
 and tsunami in Cascadia,

00:18:58.503 --> 00:19:00.272
 native peoples of this region

00:19:00.272 --> 00:19:05.177
 do hold haunting memories
 of these terrible events.

00:19:05.177 --> 00:19:07.512
GOLDFINGER: The First Nation
peoples had oral histories

00:19:07.512 --> 00:19:11.216
about the great battle
of Thunderbird and Whale

00:19:11.216 --> 00:19:13.151
that had a lot of shaking
of the ground,

00:19:13.151 --> 00:19:15.587
washing away of villages.

00:19:15.587 --> 00:19:17.222
MAN: The Great Thunderbird

00:19:17.222 --> 00:19:20.592
finally carried the Whale
to its nest.

00:19:20.592 --> 00:19:23.561
There were a shaking,
jumping up,

00:19:23.561 --> 00:19:26.298
and trembling
of the earth beneath

00:19:26.298 --> 00:19:29.368
and the rolling up
of the great waters.

00:19:29.368 --> 00:19:30.936
GOLDFINGER: So, once
these stories came to light,

00:19:30.936 --> 00:19:33.171
the Native Americans
essentially said,

00:19:33.171 --> 00:19:37.108
'Why didn't you just ask us?
We knew all about that.'

00:19:37.108 --> 00:19:38.610
The story had a moral,
and the moral was,

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:41.280
don't build your village
too close to the beach.

00:19:44.616 --> 00:19:50.188
 NARRATOR: But evidently,
 no one was listening.

00:19:50.188 --> 00:19:55.093
 Today, 10 million people live
 along the coast of Cascadia

00:19:55.093 --> 00:20:00.165
 and in the cities of Seattle,
 Portland, and Vancouver,

00:20:00.165 --> 00:20:03.167
 and the question is
 how likely is it

00:20:03.167 --> 00:20:06.505
 that the monster
 will roar again?

00:20:06.505 --> 00:20:08.407
THOMAS HEATON: It's not at all
like a clock,

00:20:08.407 --> 00:20:09.908
but the average time

00:20:09.908 --> 00:20:11.142
between the earthquakes

00:20:11.142 --> 00:20:14.379
is on the order of 400 years.

00:20:14.379 --> 00:20:16.581
 NARRATOR:
 If the Cascadia subduction zone

00:20:16.581 --> 00:20:19.551
 repeats its 400-year cycle,

00:20:19.551 --> 00:20:23.588
 then its next mega-quake
 could be imminent.

00:20:23.588 --> 00:20:25.290
O'DRISCOLL:
We know the strain is building,

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:27.225
and we know, one day,
it's gonna be released,

00:20:27.225 --> 00:20:29.294
and that could happen any day.

00:20:29.294 --> 00:20:33.364
 NARRATOR: So, what will happen
 when the monster wakes?

00:20:33.364 --> 00:20:34.966
GOLDFINGER:
In a subduction zone earthquake,

00:20:34.966 --> 00:20:38.570
energy is stretched out,
not 10 seconds or 20 seconds,

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:40.172
more like three minutes.

00:20:40.172 --> 00:20:41.173
HEATON: The shaking's not

00:20:41.173 --> 00:20:43.174
incredibly violent,

00:20:43.174 --> 00:20:44.542
but it lasts for a long time,

00:20:44.542 --> 00:20:46.345
and it's a very long,

00:20:46.345 --> 00:20:48.246
rolling motion.

00:20:48.246 --> 00:20:51.883
For tall buildings,
that's not good news.

00:20:55.487 --> 00:20:57.455
 NARRATOR:
 With buildings collapsing

00:20:57.455 --> 00:21:00.024
 and infrastructures shattered,

00:21:00.024 --> 00:21:02.627
 it will be hard
 for the population to escape

00:21:02.627 --> 00:21:05.529
 from the coastal area,

00:21:05.529 --> 00:21:10.401
 the worst place to be trapped.

00:21:10.401 --> 00:21:12.604
HEATON: You know that
if you get a giant earthquake,

00:21:12.604 --> 00:21:13.838
you'd better assume you're

00:21:13.838 --> 00:21:15.474
about to get a giant tsunami.

00:21:18.309 --> 00:21:21.513
 NARRATOR: The tectonic plates'
 sudden release of pressure

00:21:21.513 --> 00:21:26.217
 forces a massive swell
 in the ocean above.

00:21:26.217 --> 00:21:28.653
GOLDFINGER:
So, the wave height, initially,

00:21:28.653 --> 00:21:30.455
might only be
a couple of meters,

00:21:30.455 --> 00:21:32.958
but it might be
200 kilometers long,

00:21:32.958 --> 00:21:35.226
and it heads out
at the deep-water wave speed,

00:21:35.226 --> 00:21:37.329
which is very fast,
so it's jetliner speed,

00:21:37.329 --> 00:21:39.398
500 or 600 miles an hour.

00:21:41.232 --> 00:21:44.269
Everything we've seen in Japan,
we can expect, basically,

00:21:44.269 --> 00:21:46.138
the exact same thing
in Cascadia.

00:21:46.138 --> 00:21:50.274
(man on loudspeaker)

00:21:50.274 --> 00:21:53.411
HEATON:
30 meters of water rushing in

00:21:53.411 --> 00:21:57.349
at 10 meters a second
kind of velocities...

00:21:59.751 --> 00:22:00.752
...it just scours

00:22:00.752 --> 00:22:02.153
everything off the,

00:22:02.153 --> 00:22:04.022
off the surface of the earth.

00:22:05.122 --> 00:22:06.725
 NARRATOR: Within 20 minutes,

00:22:06.725 --> 00:22:10.095
 the tsunami will approach
 the coast of Cascadia.

00:22:10.095 --> 00:22:11.496
GOLDFINGER: When that wave
starts feeling bottom,

00:22:11.496 --> 00:22:12.697
like all other waves do,

00:22:12.697 --> 00:22:14.666
it gets compressed
from 200 kilometers

00:22:14.666 --> 00:22:17.001
down to a kilometer or two.

00:22:17.001 --> 00:22:18.403
HEATON: It's like an enormous

00:22:18.403 --> 00:22:21.239
tide happening very quickly.

00:22:21.239 --> 00:22:26.378
(music)

00:22:30.615 --> 00:22:33.418
 NARRATOR: A Cascadia mega-quake
 is predicted to be

00:22:33.418 --> 00:22:38.223
 North America's
 worst ever natural disaster.

00:22:38.223 --> 00:22:41.192
O'DRISCOLL: The fatalities
could be fairly large.

00:22:41.192 --> 00:22:44.095
We're looking at, at a few tens
of thousands of people,

00:22:44.095 --> 00:22:45.496
at least, on our coastline.

00:22:45.496 --> 00:22:47.499
We know the strain is building,

00:22:47.499 --> 00:22:48.933
and that's quite harrowing
to know

00:22:48.933 --> 00:22:53.138
that we could have the big one
right now, as we speak.

00:22:53.138 --> 00:22:56.340
 NARRATOR: But Cascadia is not
 the only populated region

00:22:56.340 --> 00:23:00.178
 in the Ring of Fire
 danger zone.

00:23:00.178 --> 00:23:04.215
 As the waters of the Pacific
 continue to drain away,

00:23:04.215 --> 00:23:07.352
 they reveal
 another sleeping giant

00:23:07.352 --> 00:23:10.121
 near the east coast
 of New Zealand,

00:23:10.121 --> 00:23:14.459
 where scientists discover
 traces of an epic seismic event

00:23:14.459 --> 00:23:17.562
 that reshaped
 the Southern Hemisphere

00:23:17.562 --> 00:23:22.667
 and ask,
 when will it happen again?

00:23:31.743 --> 00:23:33.744
 NARRATOR:
 The islands of New Zealand

00:23:33.744 --> 00:23:37.482
 lie directly
 on the Ring of Fire.

00:23:37.482 --> 00:23:42.720
 Small earthquakes shake
 the ground here every week.

00:23:42.720 --> 00:23:46.524
(sirens)

00:23:46.524 --> 00:23:48.226
(screaming)

00:23:48.226 --> 00:23:52.497
 In 2011, the country's second
 largest city, Christchurch,

00:23:52.497 --> 00:23:57.169
 is devastated
 by a magnitude 6.3 quake...

00:23:57.169 --> 00:23:59.537
WOMAN: I can confirm
that there have been deaths.

00:23:59.537 --> 00:24:02.440
 NARRATOR:
 ...killing 185 people.

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:04.209
(siren)

00:24:10.848 --> 00:24:13.251
 100 miles from Christchurch

00:24:13.251 --> 00:24:15.454
 is the town of Kaikoura.

00:24:17.321 --> 00:24:18.456
MAN: Kaikoura, welcome aboard.

00:24:18.456 --> 00:24:19.691
WOMAN: Thank you.

00:24:19.691 --> 00:24:21.525
 NARRATOR:
 A peaceful holiday resort

00:24:21.525 --> 00:24:23.494
 that's popular
 with nature lovers.

00:24:23.494 --> 00:24:24.896
MAN: Keep looking out there.

00:24:24.896 --> 00:24:27.531
You'll see them just
out of the front of us there.

00:24:27.531 --> 00:24:30.235
 NARRATOR:
 Its deep, nutrient-rich waters

00:24:30.235 --> 00:24:32.737
 entice whales
 close to the shore.

00:24:32.737 --> 00:24:36.273
MAN: This is
the beautiful humpback,

00:24:36.273 --> 00:24:40.278
and it is awesome to see humps.

00:24:40.278 --> 00:24:41.546
 NARRATOR:
 While these leviathans

00:24:41.546 --> 00:24:46.117
 are Kaikoura's
 star attraction,

00:24:46.117 --> 00:24:50.555
 geologists now come here
 for a very different reason.

00:24:52.523 --> 00:24:55.493
 New Zealand's geologists
 constantly monitor

00:24:55.493 --> 00:24:58.429
 the threat posed
 by the faults and fissures

00:24:58.429 --> 00:25:00.365
 that run under the islands,

00:25:00.365 --> 00:25:04.536
 trying to discover where the
 next big quake might strike.

00:25:06.671 --> 00:25:08.572
 Ground surveys can help,

00:25:08.572 --> 00:25:10.875
 but investigating
 under the waves

00:25:10.875 --> 00:25:13.578
 has always been a challenge,

00:25:13.578 --> 00:25:15.280
 until now,

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:18.716
 because the latest
 multiple-beam sonar technology

00:25:18.716 --> 00:25:22.420
 is opening up a hidden world.

00:25:22.420 --> 00:25:25.557
JOSHU MOUNTJOY: It's multiple
beams of sound energy

00:25:25.557 --> 00:25:27.958
being released from
the instrument on the vessel,

00:25:27.958 --> 00:25:31.362
and we time how long it takes
for that beam to come back.

00:25:31.362 --> 00:25:32.830
(beeping)

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:34.766
We do what we call
mowing the grass,

00:25:34.766 --> 00:25:37.501
and we're just collecting
this continuous image

00:25:37.501 --> 00:25:40.805
of what the shape
of the seabed is.

00:25:40.805 --> 00:25:43.641
 NARRATOR: And the shape
 of the seabed near Kaikoura

00:25:43.641 --> 00:25:47.946
 is not what they expected.

00:25:47.946 --> 00:25:51.216
 As the data comes in,
 it slowly reveals

00:25:51.216 --> 00:25:55.453
 something astonishing
 and potentially lethal.

00:25:58.556 --> 00:26:01.960
 Pulling the plug
 on the Pacific Ocean

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:04.529
 exposes the remarkable sight.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:13.571
 Just a half mile
 from the town of Kaikoura,

00:26:13.571 --> 00:26:16.507
 as the water drains away,

00:26:16.507 --> 00:26:21.646
 it reveals the plunging walls
 of a vast, undersea chasm...

00:26:24.582 --> 00:26:28.719
 ...the Kaikoura Canyon.

00:26:28.719 --> 00:26:29.787
MOUNTJOY: The Kaikoura Canyon,

00:26:29.787 --> 00:26:31.656
it comes within
a really short distance

00:26:31.656 --> 00:26:35.527
of the coastline out here,
less than a kilometer,

00:26:35.527 --> 00:26:38.329
and, from there,
it drops down at 30, 40 degrees

00:26:38.329 --> 00:26:41.666
down to about 600 meters
water depth.

00:26:41.666 --> 00:26:46.538
Then it carries on down to
about 2,000 meters water depth.

00:26:46.538 --> 00:26:49.573
 NARRATOR:
 Stretching for over 37 miles,

00:26:49.573 --> 00:26:51.108
 the canyon forms part

00:26:51.108 --> 00:26:54.479
 of one of the deepest
 sea channels in the world.

00:26:56.680 --> 00:27:02.720
 The walls nose-dive
 over 6,000 feet--

00:27:02.720 --> 00:27:04.823
 the depth of the Grand Canyon.

00:27:09.694 --> 00:27:12.797
 Geologists wonder at
 the immense geological forces

00:27:12.797 --> 00:27:15.900
 involved to create
 such a huge canyon...

00:27:18.936 --> 00:27:21.372
 ...but also ask themselves,

00:27:21.372 --> 00:27:25.810
 does it pose a threat to those
 living on the nearby coast?

00:27:30.415 --> 00:27:32.517
 Geologists know that landslides

00:27:32.517 --> 00:27:35.553
 triggered by earthquakes
 in underwater canyons

00:27:35.553 --> 00:27:38.956
 can generate lethal tsunamis.

00:27:38.956 --> 00:27:42.927
 Could the Kaikoura Canyon
 do the same?

00:27:42.927 --> 00:27:47.498
 On board the research ship
 Tangaroa, they investigate.

00:27:47.498 --> 00:27:48.733
MAN: Let's try it again, Mitch.

00:27:48.733 --> 00:27:51.736
(radio chatter)

00:27:51.736 --> 00:27:54.772
 NARRATOR: Using Perspex tubes,
 they gather evidence

00:27:54.772 --> 00:27:59.511
 from the sedimentary layers
 on the canyon's floor,

00:27:59.511 --> 00:28:03.581
 looking for signs of seismic
 activity in the past.

00:28:05.717 --> 00:28:06.718
MAN: Yep.

00:28:08.786 --> 00:28:11.055
MAN: This is core two.

00:28:11.055 --> 00:28:14.759
 NARRATOR: But then, just
 as they're studying history,

00:28:14.759 --> 00:28:17.662
 they get the chance
 to make some.

00:28:20.698 --> 00:28:26.137
 Midnight, November 14, 2016.

00:28:26.137 --> 00:28:27.605
(static)

00:28:27.605 --> 00:28:31.076
WOMAN: And we are undergoing
a fairly dense earthquake

00:28:31.076 --> 00:28:32.643
at the moment,

00:28:32.643 --> 00:28:34.078
so, please,
just get to somewhere

00:28:34.078 --> 00:28:38.616
where you are safely
under some protection.

00:28:38.616 --> 00:28:40.518
I can honestly say,
I doubt that I'll be able

00:28:40.518 --> 00:28:43.955
to stay in the chair
for much longer.

00:28:43.955 --> 00:28:46.624
(alarm)

00:28:50.895 --> 00:28:55.667
 NARRATOR: The 7.8 magnitude
 quake rips apart the earth,

00:28:55.667 --> 00:29:01.339
 creating giant crevices and
 billions of dollars of damage.

00:29:01.339 --> 00:29:07.846
 Geologist Joshu Mountjoy has
 never seen anything like it.

00:29:07.846 --> 00:29:09.613
MOUNTJOY: The key thing was
that it just covered

00:29:09.613 --> 00:29:11.449
such a large geographic area,

00:29:11.449 --> 00:29:13.584
and it was right
on our coastline.

00:29:13.584 --> 00:29:16.620
 NARRATOR: The quake rattles
 the ground for two minutes,

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:18.689
 causing thousands
 of landslides

00:29:18.689 --> 00:29:22.226
 across 3,800 square miles.

00:29:22.226 --> 00:29:25.863
 Just north of Kaikoura
 is the biggest.

00:29:25.863 --> 00:29:30.734
 800 million cubic feet
 has shaken to the ground,

00:29:30.734 --> 00:29:35.806
 exposing a virgin cliff face
 almost a mile wide.

00:29:35.806 --> 00:29:37.709
MOUNTJOY: Grass and tussock
we're standing on

00:29:37.709 --> 00:29:40.110
was connected to
that grass and tussock up there,

00:29:40.110 --> 00:29:42.480
and then the whole block
slid right down

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:44.982
to bring it down
to where it is now,

00:29:44.982 --> 00:29:47.084
so this has happened
during the earthquake.

00:29:47.084 --> 00:29:49.454
We call that co-seismic,
and there's actually

00:29:49.454 --> 00:29:50.855
a fault line
running through here

00:29:50.855 --> 00:29:53.591
that runs
right down to the coastline.

00:29:53.591 --> 00:29:55.059
 NARRATOR:
 The shaking is so severe

00:29:55.059 --> 00:29:57.895
 that it thrusts the foreshore
 of Kaikoura

00:29:57.895 --> 00:30:02.499
 upwards by three feet
 in just one second.

00:30:02.499 --> 00:30:03.468
MOUNTJOY: We're looking at here,

00:30:03.468 --> 00:30:04.702
we can see
a whole a lot of seaweed

00:30:04.702 --> 00:30:06.871
that's stranded
above the tide line.

00:30:06.871 --> 00:30:08.072
Before the earthquake,

00:30:08.072 --> 00:30:11.843
that would have been
down below the sea level.

00:30:11.843 --> 00:30:14.579
 NARRATOR: But Joshu Mountjoy
 isn't only interested

00:30:14.579 --> 00:30:18.449
 in how the earthquake
 tears apart the land.

00:30:18.449 --> 00:30:20.951
 As soon
 as the earthquake begins,

00:30:20.951 --> 00:30:22.920
 he wants to know
 the effect it's having

00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:25.523
 deep under the ocean,

00:30:25.523 --> 00:30:29.660
 in the newly scanned
 Kaikoura Canyon.

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:33.831
 Mountjoy makes contact with
 the research ship Tangaroa,

00:30:33.831 --> 00:30:37.468
 which is mapping
 the very far end of the canyon.

00:30:37.468 --> 00:30:39.203
MOUNTJOY: We requested
that they divert their energy

00:30:39.203 --> 00:30:42.439
to focus on doing
some earthquake response.

00:30:42.439 --> 00:30:45.810
 NARRATOR: That response is to
 immediately gather core samples

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:49.847
 from the floor of the canyon
 before the dust has settled.

00:30:49.847 --> 00:30:50.982
ALAN ORPIN:
So, we really had

00:30:50.982 --> 00:30:53.884
an unprecedented opportunity
to assist.

00:30:53.884 --> 00:30:56.788
Well, what, what is the
aftermath of a major earthquake?

00:30:56.788 --> 00:30:58.890
 NARRATOR:
 If the crew responds in time,

00:30:58.890 --> 00:31:01.892
 they can achieve a world first,

00:31:01.892 --> 00:31:04.962
 recording the impact
 of an earthquake

00:31:04.962 --> 00:31:08.833
 in a deep-sea canyon
 as it happens.

00:31:14.672 --> 00:31:16.841
 NARRATOR: Within minutes
 of the earthquake,

00:31:16.841 --> 00:31:22.246
 the crew retrieves precious
 core samples from the seafloor.

00:31:22.246 --> 00:31:24.548
MOUNTJOY: They collected
the sedimentary deposit

00:31:24.548 --> 00:31:26.951
that had only just happened,
so they were boring it

00:31:26.951 --> 00:31:29.953
while it was probably still
settling out of the ocean.

00:31:29.953 --> 00:31:33.524
 NARRATOR: And what marine
 geologist Alan Orpin discovers

00:31:33.524 --> 00:31:36.527
 is intriguing.

00:31:36.527 --> 00:31:38.930
ORPIN: What we found was
a very soupy, fluidized mud

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:41.965
that was lying
on the seabed.

00:31:41.965 --> 00:31:43.000
As we moved south,

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:45.236
what we found
was compelling evidence

00:31:45.236 --> 00:31:49.039
of core after core
for a thickness of material

00:31:49.039 --> 00:31:51.074
that had spilled down
through the channel.

00:31:51.074 --> 00:31:52.976
 NARRATOR:
 The more samples they take,

00:31:52.976 --> 00:31:55.513
 the clearer
 the picture becomes.

00:31:55.513 --> 00:31:58.816
 The shaking of the quake
 has created a secondary

00:31:58.816 --> 00:32:02.987
 and truly spectacular
 undersea upheaval.

00:32:02.987 --> 00:32:05.256
MOUNTJOY: No one's ever
been able to observe one

00:32:05.256 --> 00:32:09.293
of these huge landscape
scale canyon-flushing events

00:32:09.293 --> 00:32:11.195
to show exactly
what effect it had

00:32:11.195 --> 00:32:12.963
on the canyon itself.

00:32:12.963 --> 00:32:17.468
(music)

00:32:17.468 --> 00:32:18.669
 NARRATOR: Using the data

00:32:18.669 --> 00:32:21.005
 gathered by the crew
 of Tangaroa

00:32:21.005 --> 00:32:23.841
 and the remarkable
 drained seascape,

00:32:23.841 --> 00:32:26.077
 the impact
 of a large earthquake

00:32:26.077 --> 00:32:28.946
 on a gigantic underwater canyon

00:32:28.946 --> 00:32:34.718
 can now be visualized
 for the very first time.

00:32:34.718 --> 00:32:39.723
 The seabed shudders ominously
 as huge tectonic forces

00:32:39.723 --> 00:32:41.826
 power through
 the earth's crust.

00:32:41.826 --> 00:32:43.894
(rumbling)

00:32:43.894 --> 00:32:48.366
 This dislodges
 a top layer of sediment,

00:32:48.366 --> 00:32:53.004
 becoming a gigantic
 canyon-flushing landslide.

00:32:53.004 --> 00:32:57.575
(rumbling)

00:32:57.575 --> 00:33:01.579
(music)

00:33:01.579 --> 00:33:04.882
 Rock and sediment
 tumble down the steep walls.

00:33:09.086 --> 00:33:14.225
 850 million tons of debris
 roars through the canyon,

00:33:14.225 --> 00:33:19.263
 gouging 160 feet
 out of the floor.

00:33:19.263 --> 00:33:21.933
MOUNTJOY: There's really like
a powder snow avalanche.

00:33:24.102 --> 00:33:26.937
The front of it would have
a huge amount of energy

00:33:26.937 --> 00:33:29.040
moving at about
70 kilometers an hour

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:31.242
and maybe 400 meters high.

00:33:35.046 --> 00:33:37.281
It's spreading
tens of kilometers

00:33:37.281 --> 00:33:40.251
across the ocean floor.

00:33:40.251 --> 00:33:44.088
It's just gonna take over
everything that's down there.

00:33:44.088 --> 00:33:47.057
 NARRATOR: The landslide travels
 with such velocity,

00:33:47.057 --> 00:33:50.127
 it carves a path
 across the seafloor

00:33:50.127 --> 00:33:54.932
 for an astonishing 435 miles.

00:34:00.938 --> 00:34:04.709
 A landslide of this ferocity
 has the potential

00:34:04.709 --> 00:34:10.114
 to generate a tsunami
 that could devastate Kaikoura.

00:34:10.114 --> 00:34:12.082
MOUNTJOY: When a landslide
creates a tsunami,

00:34:12.082 --> 00:34:15.719
you need a large chunk
of material to slide downslope,

00:34:15.719 --> 00:34:18.088
and what happens is it's
coupled to the water above it

00:34:18.088 --> 00:34:19.957
and actually
pulls it down with it,

00:34:19.957 --> 00:34:21.325
and then,
as that water rebounds,

00:34:21.325 --> 00:34:24.161
then you create your wave.

00:34:24.161 --> 00:34:26.998
 NARRATOR:
 This time, Kaikoura is lucky.

00:34:26.998 --> 00:34:30.868
 No large objects
 fall off the canyon rim.

00:34:30.868 --> 00:34:34.272
 Next time, the town may not
 be so fortunate.

00:34:37.007 --> 00:34:42.312
 And the dramatic events of 2016
 draw the geologists' attention

00:34:42.312 --> 00:34:45.983
 to an even bigger
 tsunami threat nearby.

00:34:48.085 --> 00:34:50.321
 By continuing
 to drain the ocean

00:34:50.321 --> 00:34:52.990
 off the northeast coast
 of New Zealand,

00:34:52.990 --> 00:34:56.393
 we can see the reason
 for their fear.

00:34:56.393 --> 00:34:59.029
 Lurking just north
 of the canyon

00:34:59.029 --> 00:35:00.364
 and frighteningly close

00:35:00.364 --> 00:35:04.501
 to the New Zealand capital
 of Wellington--

00:35:04.501 --> 00:35:08.105
 the Hikurangi subduction zone,

00:35:08.105 --> 00:35:13.444
 2,174 miles
 of seismic trouble.

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:20.551
 Geologists recently learned
 that stress has been building

00:35:20.551 --> 00:35:26.057
 at its southern end
 for hundreds of years,

00:35:26.057 --> 00:35:29.226
 and they believe
 that the 2016 earthquake

00:35:29.226 --> 00:35:32.730
 has ratcheted the pressure
 up even further.

00:35:36.100 --> 00:35:38.335
 Understanding
 what's happening here

00:35:38.335 --> 00:35:42.840
 has become
 an international priority.

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:46.109
 100 ocean floor
 seismic measuring devices

00:35:46.109 --> 00:35:51.515
 and support scientists
 recently arrived from Japan.

00:35:51.515 --> 00:35:55.119
 They're here to monitor and
 record the mounting stresses

00:35:55.119 --> 00:36:00.524
 in the hope of discovering
 the true danger of Hikurangi.

00:36:00.524 --> 00:36:05.629
(music)

00:36:05.629 --> 00:36:07.264
 Geologists delve deep

00:36:07.264 --> 00:36:10.401
 into the subduction zone's
 tectonic past.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:17.141
 And their results
 are astounding.

00:36:17.141 --> 00:36:21.311
 It hasn't fractured recently,
 but deep in its past,

00:36:21.311 --> 00:36:26.249
 it produced something bigger
 than anyone had imagined.

00:36:26.249 --> 00:36:29.253
 Now, using
 the geologists' data,

00:36:29.253 --> 00:36:33.257
 it's possible to reconstruct,
 for the first time,

00:36:33.257 --> 00:36:38.929
 a tectonic event
 that reshaped a hemisphere--

00:36:38.929 --> 00:36:42.499
 the Ruatoria avalanche.

00:36:42.499 --> 00:36:45.870
 Just 200 miles from
 what today is New Zealand's

00:36:45.870 --> 00:36:49.173
 bustling capital, Wellington,

00:36:49.173 --> 00:36:52.375
 the epicenter of one
 of the biggest seismic events

00:36:52.375 --> 00:36:54.945
 in the history of the earth.

00:36:54.945 --> 00:36:56.847
(rumbling)

00:36:56.847 --> 00:37:01.251
 170,000 years ago,
 the Australian plate

00:37:01.251 --> 00:37:04.288
 is bearing down
 on its Pacific neighbor,

00:37:04.288 --> 00:37:07.191
 grinding down
 everything in its path.

00:37:07.191 --> 00:37:10.027
 Even a towering
 volcanic cone

00:37:10.027 --> 00:37:12.496
 six times the volume
 of Mount Fuji

00:37:12.496 --> 00:37:15.132
 becomes collateral damage.

00:37:18.201 --> 00:37:20.571
 The resulting seismic shock

00:37:20.571 --> 00:37:23.106
 triggers
 an underwater landslide

00:37:23.106 --> 00:37:25.575
 on a scale rarely seen.

00:37:25.575 --> 00:37:28.211
 Moving a swath of broken rock,

00:37:28.211 --> 00:37:31.115
 the equivalent
 of three-quarters the size

00:37:31.115 --> 00:37:33.150
 of the Grand Canyon.

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:34.218
(rumbling)

00:37:34.218 --> 00:37:40.657
(rumbling)

00:37:40.657 --> 00:37:41.958
MOUNTJOY:
So, the Ruatoria avalanche

00:37:41.958 --> 00:37:44.328
is the second biggest submarine
landslide in the world.

00:37:44.328 --> 00:37:47.331
It's absolutely enormous,
3,000 cubic kilometers.

00:37:47.331 --> 00:37:49.433
And if you can try and visualize
a cubic kilometer,

00:37:49.433 --> 00:37:51.068
that's a kilometer high

00:37:51.068 --> 00:37:53.237
by a kilometer long
by a kilometer wide,

00:37:53.237 --> 00:37:55.205
3,000 of them.

00:37:55.205 --> 00:38:01.211
(music)

00:38:01.211 --> 00:38:04.314
 NARRATOR: Nobody knows when
 the Hikurangi subduction zone

00:38:04.314 --> 00:38:07.218
 will facture again,

00:38:07.218 --> 00:38:08.718
 but when it does,

00:38:08.718 --> 00:38:12.389
 geologists estimate the tsunami
 wave from the mega-quake

00:38:12.389 --> 00:38:14.458
 will take only seven minutes

00:38:14.458 --> 00:38:18.195
 to reach New Zealand's
 capital city, Wellington,

00:38:18.195 --> 00:38:20.331
 and its
 half a million residents.

00:38:25.602 --> 00:38:27.337
 And such a huge tsunami

00:38:27.337 --> 00:38:31.074
 wouldn't only impact
 New Zealand.

00:38:31.074 --> 00:38:33.376
 There would be
 an ocean-wide alert

00:38:33.376 --> 00:38:36.180
 as the waves fan out
 into the Pacific.

00:38:39.750 --> 00:38:42.986
(screaming)

00:38:42.986 --> 00:38:45.256
 The last time a tsunami
 on this scale

00:38:45.256 --> 00:38:47.758
 hit the southern arc
 of the Ring of Fire

00:38:47.758 --> 00:38:52.763
 was December 26, 2004,

00:38:52.763 --> 00:38:56.599
 devastating 14 countries

00:38:56.599 --> 00:39:02.272
 and killing 230,000 people--

00:39:02.272 --> 00:39:06.644
 a terrible reminder of the
 power of the deadly Pacific.

00:39:10.414 --> 00:39:13.350
 Continuing to drain away
 the Pacific Ocean

00:39:13.350 --> 00:39:17.454
 reveals a very different kind
 of seismic threat

00:39:17.454 --> 00:39:21.591
 5,000 miles away,

00:39:21.591 --> 00:39:25.629
 right next to one of the most
 densely populated cities

00:39:25.629 --> 00:39:27.531
 on the planet.

00:39:27.531 --> 00:39:31.101
 Just 44 miles
 from bustling Tokyo,

00:39:31.101 --> 00:39:33.537
 a mysterious underwater feature

00:39:33.537 --> 00:39:37.007
 has scientists
 on the edge of their seats.

00:39:41.311 --> 00:39:43.280
 The island nation of Japan

00:39:43.280 --> 00:39:48.385
 suffers from its position
 on the Ring of Fire.

00:39:48.385 --> 00:39:50.721
 The country accounts
 for about 20 percent

00:39:50.721 --> 00:39:55.592
 of the world's earthquakes
 of magnitude 6 or greater.

00:39:55.592 --> 00:39:57.761
 Its biggest city, Tokyo,

00:39:57.761 --> 00:40:00.497
 has been hit
 by nine mega-earthquakes

00:40:00.497 --> 00:40:04.402
 in the past 95 years.

00:40:04.402 --> 00:40:08.172
 But just 44 miles
 off the city's shoreline,

00:40:08.172 --> 00:40:10.374
 geologists
 have recently discovered

00:40:10.374 --> 00:40:14.345
 a new and very different
 tectonic troublemaker.

00:40:21.451 --> 00:40:26.724
 The first discovery is made
 by scientist Ken Tani.

00:40:26.724 --> 00:40:28.325
 He's heading out to test

00:40:28.325 --> 00:40:33.263
 his new underwater remotely
 operated vehicle, or ROV,

00:40:33.263 --> 00:40:36.366
 in the waters
 just off the island of Oshima,

00:40:36.366 --> 00:40:38.568
 south of Tokyo Bay.

00:40:38.568 --> 00:40:43.206
 Until now, the waters here have
 been considered safe passage

00:40:43.206 --> 00:40:45.175
 for one of the busiest
 shipping lanes

00:40:45.175 --> 00:40:47.177
 into the port of Tokyo,

00:40:47.177 --> 00:40:51.414
 with 400 vessels
 passing through here every day,

00:40:51.414 --> 00:40:54.385
 but that is about to change.

00:41:03.727 --> 00:41:06.496
 NARRATOR:
 44 miles from Tokyo Bay,

00:41:06.496 --> 00:41:11.468
 Ken Tani prepares an underwater
 survey, using an ROV.

00:41:11.468 --> 00:41:15.138
(speaking Japanese)

00:41:15.138 --> 00:41:18.575
 Passing over a rocky mound
 on the seafloor,

00:41:18.575 --> 00:41:22.246
 he discovers
 something extraordinary,

00:41:22.246 --> 00:41:25.515
 a seismic beast
 that has lain hidden

00:41:25.515 --> 00:41:30.187
 for hundreds of thousands
 of years.

00:41:30.187 --> 00:41:35.125
 Using Ken Tani's own data,
 draining away the Pacific Ocean

00:41:35.125 --> 00:41:38.295
 reveals what is lurking
 below the waves.

00:41:46.669 --> 00:41:48.872
 As the water recedes,

00:41:48.872 --> 00:41:54.044
 it exposes
 a large flat plateau.

00:41:54.044 --> 00:41:55.579
 Near its center,

00:41:55.579 --> 00:41:58.716
 what looks like an ordinary
 hole in the seafloor.

00:42:01.484 --> 00:42:06.456
 But on closer inspection,
 the frightening truth emerges.

00:42:06.456 --> 00:42:10.294
 It's the vent
 of an undersea volcano.

00:42:12.595 --> 00:42:14.464
 Thousands of years ago,

00:42:14.464 --> 00:42:16.934
 it very likely
 had a towering cone,

00:42:16.934 --> 00:42:20.537
 but erosion and wave action
 has worn it down,

00:42:20.537 --> 00:42:23.340
 which is why
 no one discovered it before.

00:42:25.642 --> 00:42:30.680
 But is it as dormant
 as it appears?

00:42:30.680 --> 00:42:34.184
 Ken wastes no time
 in exploring the crater.

00:42:34.184 --> 00:42:36.319
KEN TANI:
So, that's the sea surface.

00:42:36.319 --> 00:42:38.522
 NARRATOR: Testing the water
 temperature around the outlet

00:42:38.522 --> 00:42:40.957
 with a submarine thermometer,

00:42:40.957 --> 00:42:44.495
 and what he finds
 surprises everyone.

00:43:03.680 --> 00:43:10.020
(music)

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:12.589
 NARRATOR:
 The smoldering undersea volcano

00:43:12.589 --> 00:43:14.858
 is named Oomurodashi,

00:43:14.858 --> 00:43:19.463
 44 miles south of Tokyo,

00:43:19.463 --> 00:43:22.865
 placing at risk the lives
 of thousands of mariners

00:43:22.865 --> 00:43:24.768
 in the shipping lanes above.

00:43:27.705 --> 00:43:32.642
 In 1952, an underwater volcano
 like Oomurodashi

00:43:32.642 --> 00:43:36.746
 erupts 260 miles
 south of Tokyo.

00:43:36.746 --> 00:43:39.950
 Super-heated magma
 reacts with water

00:43:39.950 --> 00:43:43.887
 to produce what's called
 a phreatomagmatic explosion,

00:43:43.887 --> 00:43:48.025
 an underwater steam bomb.

00:43:48.025 --> 00:43:51.628
 A nearby ship is engulfed
 in the explosion,

00:43:51.628 --> 00:43:54.231
 killing all 31 on board.

00:44:09.679 --> 00:44:11.682
 NARRATOR:
 A stirring volcanic giant

00:44:11.682 --> 00:44:15.085
 is not just
 a danger to shipping.

00:44:15.085 --> 00:44:18.388
 A major eruption
 could send tsunami waves

00:44:18.388 --> 00:44:23.727
 racing towards Tokyo,
 population 38 million.

00:44:32.035 --> 00:44:33.403
 NARRATOR: Ken decides he needs

00:44:33.403 --> 00:44:36.273
 to keep Oomurodashi
 under surveillance.

00:44:38.442 --> 00:44:41.611
 Nothing happens for five years.

00:44:41.611 --> 00:44:45.315
 Then, when he returns
 to gather fresh samples,

00:44:45.315 --> 00:44:49.219
 Oomurodashi has another
 unpleasant surprise for him.

00:44:51.388 --> 00:44:53.857
 Returning
 to the drained ocean floor

00:44:53.857 --> 00:44:57.627
 reveals his next
 shocking discovery.

00:44:57.627 --> 00:44:59.929
 In addition to the crater,

00:44:59.929 --> 00:45:03.734
 five more volcanic cones.

00:45:03.734 --> 00:45:08.705
 That's five more possible
 outlets for the volcano.

00:45:08.705 --> 00:45:13.944
 Only one is currently active,
 but all could become so.

00:45:13.944 --> 00:45:17.381
 And if the volcano
 keeps growing,

00:45:17.381 --> 00:45:20.817
 Oomurodashi could become
 an even greater threat

00:45:20.817 --> 00:45:22.819
 by breaking the surface

00:45:22.819 --> 00:45:25.556
 and spewing toxic ash
 into the sky.

00:45:27.857 --> 00:45:32.796
 Active volcanos can do this
 astonishingly quickly.

00:45:32.796 --> 00:45:37.100
 It took Nishinoshima,
 Japan's latest volcanic island,

00:45:37.100 --> 00:45:38.835
 just two years to grow

00:45:38.835 --> 00:45:43.840
 from a flat-topped,
 underwater summit

00:45:43.840 --> 00:45:49.079
 to this tectonic monster,

00:45:49.079 --> 00:45:54.017
 measuring one square mile
 and 443 feet high.

00:45:55.785 --> 00:45:56.853
 But Nishinoshima

00:45:56.853 --> 00:45:59.822
 is nearly 600 miles from Tokyo.

00:45:59.822 --> 00:46:03.660
 Oomurodashi is only 44.

00:46:04.728 --> 00:46:06.496
 Once out of the water,

00:46:06.496 --> 00:46:08.832
 any major eruption
 of Oomurodashi

00:46:08.832 --> 00:46:14.104
 could shower Tokyo
 in choking volcanic ash.

00:46:14.104 --> 00:46:17.741
 Despite the dangers,
 Ken Tani and his team

00:46:17.741 --> 00:46:21.210
 continue
 to monitor Oomurodashi.

00:46:21.210 --> 00:46:24.581
 Any increase
 in the volcano's activity

00:46:24.581 --> 00:46:26.883
 could signal
 impending disaster

00:46:26.883 --> 00:46:31.455
 for the thousands living
 on nearby islands

00:46:31.455 --> 00:46:35.626
 and threaten the safety
 of millions in nearby Tokyo.

00:46:38.161 --> 00:46:39.962
 Draining the Ring of Fire

00:46:39.962 --> 00:46:44.601
 has revealed
 hidden geological wonders

00:46:44.601 --> 00:46:46.904
 and terrors,

00:46:46.904 --> 00:46:50.007
 exposing new evidence
 of this immense power

00:46:50.007 --> 00:46:52.976
 of plate tectonics

00:46:52.976 --> 00:46:57.748
 and its ability to create

00:46:57.748 --> 00:46:59.383
 and destroy.

